 Electronic Engineering include:
- advanced design;
- applied and industrial research;
- product and process innovation.
The following can be cited as application areas:
- microelectronic technologies, which allow the integration of an increasing number of functions in circuits with reduced surface area and decreasing consumption; the evolution of these technologies is of considerable importance, so much so as to decisively influence the processing capacity of information, and therefore the power of applications;
- nanoelectronic technologies, which aim to make a brilliant leap in quality, taking inspiration from the achievement of the physical limits of microelectronics, in order to introduce new generations of devices and therefore functions;
- optical technologies, now indispensable for the "mass" transmission of information, but rich in applications in many fields;
- measurement and monitoring instrumentation, crucially linked to the "hardware" quality of its circuit components, with applications in almost all technological fields;
- remote sensing, significantly based on the ability to process electromagnetic data and on the knowledge of the physical structure of the transmission medium and the objects to be detected;
- high frequency (RF and microwave) techniques, used not only in all radio communication systems, both fixed and mobile, but also in industrial, medical and scientific applications;
- the study of electromagnetic interference, crucial both in the design of compact circuits and high-speed information transmission systems, and in the integration of different systems, all based on the electrical nature of operation, and coexisting in the same environment;
- sensor technology, a rapidly growing sector, which essentially integrates the sensor itself with the electronics necessary for the correct interpretation and transmission of the data detected.

